# Comment Prose

Do not add a comment for anything the code already tells the reader. When a
comment is warranted, write it as a description of the code as it is,
addressed to the next maintainer — never as instructions, recommendations,
or notes to a reviewer.

Apply this when writing or editing comments, doc comments, and test prose
that will remain in the tree after the change merges.

## Tense and time

Use the indicative present. State what the code does and what constrains it.
Avoid the imperative: "fail closed when the header is unsigned" leaves the
reader unsure whether the code already does that; "this errors when the
header is unsigned" does not.

Never leave a statement that can't be placed in time. Tenseless assertions
are the worst offenders — "splitting this enum is the real fix" could
describe the code in front of you, code elsewhere, or a wish. If you are
describing a possible future state, mark it as one and say why it is not
the current state.

## What stays out of the file

Keep design opinions about work you did not do out of the file. If it is
tracked, write `TODO(TICKET-123)`. If it is not tracked, it belongs in the
pull request discussion, not in a comment that outlives it.

Do not write provenance. Never name the test that measured a fact, the
ticket that prompted the change, or the argument that convinced a reviewer.
State the constraint and drop where it came from.

Do not narrate. If a comment restates what the next line plainly does,
delete it.

## Ambiguity and systems you do not control

When a comment or a test covers more than one possible state, say which
one actually occurs. That is usually the most useful sentence available
and the one most often omitted.

Mark facts about systems you do not control as observations that may
drift: "the service signs only `host` today", not "the service signs only
`host`".

Counterfactuals and conditionals are fine when that is genuinely what you
mean — "the probe would be wasted because the endpoint cannot be swapped"
is correct. Do not flatten those into false present-tense claims while
applying the rules above.

## Keep / delete

Before keeping any comment, check that it tells the reader something the
code does not, and that it would still be accurate a year after the change
merged. If it only makes sense to someone reading the diff, delete it.
Prefer deleting to rewriting: a comment that restates the code does not
become useful by being put in the present tense.

